    "created_at": "2016-07-01T19:06:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-07-01T19:06:52Z",
    "body": "> How was this not needed for service workers?\n\nService workers got away with modifying the request and validating the response. We could, I guess, roll both of those hooks up into the fetch hook. That might be better, although I would be annoyed at causing us to churn under the service worker people again. (Also, they only want to apply their hooks to the top-level fetches.)\n\n> I basically wonder if we got the layering right or if we're working around a problem by just adding more hooks everywhere. It's starting to get increasingly more complicated how this works.\n\nThis is a fair worry. I am kind of worried too. Hmm. But I am not sure what a good solution would be. I don't think you're suggested line of inquiry is correct:\n\n> I also wonder a bit about the discussion in the other thread. It seems to me that worklets is the first platform instance of having multiple independent globals per realm. So maybe the module map is associated with realms, but the scripts run with a particular global.\n\nI don't believe worklets have multiple globals per realm. The 1:1 realm to global correspondence is preserved. (I think a lot would break if we changed that.)\n\nStill, we could envision trying to decouple the module map so it can live for longer than the lifetime of a single global/realm/settings object. The problem with that is that HTML \"scripts\" are very much tied to a specific global/realm/settings object. You'd have to create a parallel concept of \"unbound script\" (probably mostly just source text) which gets reified into a \"per-global script\". That seems like it would be worse.\n\nI guess after realizing that everything should be doable with just the fetch hook (plus maybe a boolean about whether it's a top-level fetch or not), I am less worried about the layering. There is still a bit of a mess here in having three hooks instead of one. Maybe I should file a follow-up bug to consolidate them. As long as I do all the work of pull-requesting the service worker spec, I will feel less bad about the churn.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
